20 norm); lean under on his rushing line.</p><p><strong>Trey McBride</strong> has room to work \u2014 SEA allow <strong>42 yds/game to TE1s</strong> (vs ~35), a receiving-yards and target angle.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> faces a tough cover \u2014 SEA hold WR1s to just 48 yds/game (vs ~58); a rec-yards under lean.</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>AJ Barner</strong> is a live anytime-TD dart \u2014 ARI are leaky to tight ends in the end zone (0.32 TE TDs/game vs ~0.24).</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 ARI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone ARI throw it (59% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Michael Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p>Fade the ARI run \u2014 as 10-point dogs projected to chase, <strong>James Conner</strong>'s carries get squeezed, though his receiving work ticks up in catch-up mode.</p><p>A middling 44 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>SEA \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>SEA are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (43% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p><strong>Jadarian Price</strong> (12 carries, 52 yards projected) gets a mild game-script tailwind as a favorite, though SEA's willingness to keep throwing when ahead caps how much the lead converts into extra carries.</p><p><strong>Jaxon Smith-Njigba</strong> projects for <strong>9 targets and 79 yards</strong>, in line with his established role in a script that neither inflates nor suppresses the pass.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>AJ Barner</strong> (TE, 3 targets, 26 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>ARI \u2014 a <em>balanced</em> offense</h3><p>ARI are a balanced offense \u2014 their run/pass split by game state tracks the league closely (45% pass when leading vs 44% average), so their players' usage is driven more by role than by any strong situational lean.</p><p>Game script works against the run here \u2014 ARI project to trail and throw, which squeezes <strong>James Conner</strong>'s rushing projection (11 carries, 47 yards) but <em>raises</em> his value as a receiver; trailing teams check the ball down, and his target share ticks up as the game gets away from them.</p><p><strong>Michael Wilson</strong> is the one to target from a volume standpoint: as underdogs throwing to keep pace, ARI funnel work to their top receiver \u2014 <strong>8 targets for 70 yards</strong> projected, with genuine upside if they fall behind early and the game turns one-dimensional.</p><p>Underneath, <strong>Trey McBride</strong> (TE, 11 targets, 86 yards) is the dependable secondary read \u2014 and note the red zone stays TE-friendly regardless of score, so his touchdown equity holds up even if the game script shifts.</p><h3>What to watch</h3><p>The single biggest swing factor is <strong>availability</strong> \u2014 use the injury tool in the sidebar to mark anyone out; a starter's absence doesn't just remove his line, it <em>redistributes</em> his targets and carries to specific teammates, and the report and projections rebuild around it. Beyond that: if this game breaks toward the projected 10-point margin, the <strong>garbage-time backups</strong> (the \u25c6-flagged rows below) are where soft lines and real edge tend to appear \u2014 those depth players get fed in blowouts, and the market is slowest to price them.</p><p><em>This preview and the projections below regenerate through the week as injuries, weather and line moves update the inputs.</em></p></div>","teams":[{"team":"SEA","col":"#69BE28","players":[{"name":"Sam Darnold","pos":"QB","starter":true,"min